WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.454
In 2014, Canadian law enforcement made over one point two million requests to telecommunications companies for subscriber data — without warrants.

00:00:08.454 --> 00:00:23.874
Four major surveillance bills were tabled between 2022 and 2024: Bill C-11 the Online Streaming Act, Bill C-18, Bill C-26 the Critical Cyber Systems Protection Act, and Bill C-63 the Online Harms Act.

00:00:23.874 --> 00:00:33.943
Bill C-63 increased the maximum sentence for advocating genocide from five years to life imprisonment — while simultaneously creating pre-crime bail provisions.

00:00:33.943 --> 00:00:37.909
The Privacy Act has not been meaningfully updated in over thirty years.

00:00:37.909 --> 00:00:40.443
CSIS was created in 1984.

00:00:40.443 --> 00:00:44.465
Bill C-51 in 2015 expanded its mandate.

00:00:44.465 --> 00:00:50.659
Bill C-26 allows secret government orders to internet service providers with no public disclosure.

00:00:50.659 --> 00:00:54.579
Canada is a founding member of the Five Eyes intelligence alliance.

00:00:54.579 --> 00:00:57.738
The border agency conducts warrantless device searches.

00:00:57.738 --> 00:01:01.204
The legislative program is not protecting Canadians from harm.

00:01:01.204 --> 00:01:06.818
It is constructing a comprehensive surveillance and content-control architecture — one bill at a time.
